cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "WiFi-Radar-for-SLAM: Ambient WiFi as a Cheap Radar for Automotive SLAM (simulation + ESP32 hardware)"
abstract: >-
  A physics-based (Sionna RT ray-traced) study, extended with real ESP32-S3
  hardware, of using ambient sub-7 GHz WiFi received on a moving vehicle as a
  radar/LiDAR-replacement front-end for SLAM. Vehicle localization is
  centimetre-level with a clean bandwidth/SNR operating envelope. Mapping, however,
  hits a ceiling: with realistic commodity CSI, most detections are phantoms
  (~89 %) plus a several-metre range bias - a front-end and geometry limit, not a
  path-discrimination or resolution limit. Comparisons against real LiDAR (KITTI)
  and a simulated 77 GHz FMCW radar show the ceiling is neither carrier-specific nor
  WiFi-specific: the radio carrier does not matter, geometry does (a monostatic,
  on-vehicle transmitter nearly eliminates the phantoms), and wider bandwidth makes
  them worse. The phantom-rate claim is then reproduced on ~$30 of real ESP32-S3
  silicon (HT40 CSI captured, parsed, and delay-profiled against a tape measure).
  Consolidates four component studies into one body of work.
